WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:06.920
Mission: Impossible Fallout runs for an amazing
147 minutes and is jam-packed with awesome

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.389
action scenes and stunning moments, but there
were even more great scenes cut from the final

00:00:11.389 --> 00:00:12.389
movie.

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:15.999
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video I'm revealing the top 10

00:00:15.999 --> 00:00:19.990
deleted and censored scenes you never got
to see in Mission: Impossible 6, including

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:23.500
White Widow in Kashmir, fake trailer shots,
and helicopter near-collisions.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.660
Watch out for spoilers if you haven't seen
the movie, or add this video to your Water

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.600
Later playlist and come back when you're ready.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.219
One scene from the trailers which fans were
really hoping to see but which didn't make

00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:38.670
it into the final movie was where Ethan's
helicopter was going full pelt into an on-coming

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.129
truck on a forest road.

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:45.640
This scene would have appeared during the
already-packed helicopter chase sequence between

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.300
Ethan and Henry Cavill's Walker, and I think
could have been during one of the moments

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.450
where Ethan's chopper was spiralling out of
control.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:56.010
Given how close the chopper and truck come
to colliding, I'd have love to have seen how

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.640
Ethan got himself out of this one!

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.809
In the movie, after the HALO jump in Paris,
we see Cruise and Cavill land on the roof

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:07.640
of the Grand Palais, but a further stunt sequence
filmed inside the Palais was cut.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.619
The deleted scene featured Cruise swinging
from a rope over the crowd and was rejected

00:01:11.619 --> 00:01:15.500
by writer-director Christopher McQuarrie as,
although it looked pretty cool, the film was

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.050
already running long and it wasn't necessary
for the story.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.549
While the movie gives us a number of big fight
scenes inside the Grand Palais, another smaller

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.530
one that happened outside the venue was given
the chop.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:30.950
In the final film, after Ethan and White Widow
exit the building, next thing we know they're

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.010
in the back of a car together.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:36.789
But there's a deleted scene before that car
ride where Ethan gets into a fight with White

00:01:36.789 --> 00:01:41.390
Widow's brother Zola and smashes his head
against a car, which actually left actor Frederick

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.000
Schmidt with a black eye!

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.780
I imagine this moment was ditched in the editing
room as it didn't move the plot forward but

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.500
merely reinforced the animosity we'd already
witnessed earlier when Zola warned Ethan not

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.539
to touch his sister.

00:01:53.539 --> 00:01:57.850
Actress Vanessa Kirby told MTV that the car
ride with White Widow and Ethan was part of

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.690
a much longer and now deleted scene where,
in her words, "things got a bit fruity" between

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:03.690
them.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.960
In the cut scene, White Widow talked about
how she enjoyed having blood on her dress

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.360
from the knife fight earlier in the Palais.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.360
This deleted scene would have further played
up the flirtation between her and Ethan, and

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.340
would also have been a call-back to the car
ride that Ethan took with Max in the first

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.860
Mission: Impossible movie.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.591
In case you missed it, Max was revealed to
be White Widow's mother in this film, and

00:02:22.591 --> 00:02:27.860
I explain that in more detail in my Mission:
Impossible 6 Connections and Ending video.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.650
This gorgeous scene of Ethan, White Widow
and Walker marching through a hall of mirrors

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:36.390
was in the movie, however, an alternate shot
of Ethan and Ilsa walking down the hallway

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.760
was never used.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.451
McQuarrie has said that shot of Tom Cruise
and Rebecca Ferguson was never meant to be

00:02:41.451 --> 00:02:45.290
in the movie and was specifically filmed as
something cool for the trailers.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.150
There was also a big fight scene with Isla
cut from the film, and hopefully we'll get

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:51.870
more details on that on the Blu-ray.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:56.110
The trailers also teased numerous shots from
the final act set in Kashmir which never made

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:57.260
it into the final film.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:02.349
Some of those shots hint at some pretty dark
moments with Ethan, Ilsa, Benji and Luther

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:05.560
encountering murdered soldiers and aid workers
at a border checkpoint.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.060
The scene seems to go on with Ethan approaching
the door to a bunker, opening it and descending

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.760
some stairs where I suspect he would have
discovered further horrors.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.360
It's unclear whether the murders they discover
are the actions of Walker or Solomon Lane.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.849
Some Indian viewers suspect these specific
border scenes might have been cut to avoid

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:26.480
controversy in India as the Kashmir region
which geographically spans India, Pakistan

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.099
and China is one of the most militarised areas
of the world and has long-running border and

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:32.200
separatist conflicts.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:37.189
In fact, Mission: Impossible Fallout has already
been censored by India's film classification

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:38.189
board.

00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:42.000
All mentions of Kashmir have been cut from
the Indian release of the movie, including

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.200
dialogue, the title card that comes on screen
when the region first appears, and other scenes

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.599
have also been trimmed.

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:51.489
Director Christopher McQuarrie has said that
any deleted trailer scenes were simply cut

00:03:51.489 --> 00:03:55.489
because the movie was too long, which sounds
perfectly reasonable given the film's long

00:03:55.489 --> 00:04:00.569
run-time; though I do wonder if in this instance
political considerations also played a part.

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:04.549
There's some curious behind-the-scenes footage
of White Widow actress Vanessa Kirby on the

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:07.450
set in New Zealand where the Kashmir scenes
were filmed.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.519
White Widow never appeared in Kashmir in the
final film, so I wonder whether there was

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:16.220
originally a story where the handover of Solomon
Lane that she brokered was going to take place

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.250
there.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.900
One of the more meta moments in Fallout comes
when Henry Cavill's Walker angrily asks Solomon

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.600
Lane, "why do you have to make everything
so complicated?"

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.550
Originally, McQuarrie planned for this line
to be part of a much longer segment where

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:32.780
everything in the movie was explained; Walker's
backstory, what had happened to the plutonium,

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:33.780
and so on.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.050
But test audiences hated it and thought it
was tedious, so it was cut.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:41.300
I can understand why this exposition was removed,
as fans enjoy the Mission: Impossible movies

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:45.730
not only for their incredible action scenes
but also for their twists and turns.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:49.550
These work best when the audience isn't too
confused, but doesn't know everything that's

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.180
going on and so it's about getting that balance
right.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.260
Curiously absent from the IMF team this time
around after having appeared in the previous

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:59.570
two movies was Jeremy Renner's William Brandt.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:03.501
In an interview with Digital Spy, McQuarrie
explained that "when the movie started, we

00:05:03.501 --> 00:05:07.300
didn't really have a screenplay, so it was
very difficult for us to say who would be

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.540
in the movie for how long and on what days,
and [Renner] had a commitment to Marvel."

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:15.210
"If we had a finished script, we would have
been able to say, 'Yes, this will work and

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.860
we can let you go for this time', but there
was just no predicting what those roles were

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.170
going to turn out to be."

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:22.710
"It was just an unfortunate case of bad timing."

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:26.889
Admittedly, Renner's role in Rogue Nation
was scaled back to a smaller supporting part

00:05:26.889 --> 00:05:30.789
than the one he had when he started the franchise
in Ghost Protocol, so it seems natural he

00:05:30.789 --> 00:05:34.340
would have given priority to Marvel, and,
of course, he'd obviously have to honour the

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:35.880
contract he signed with them.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.050
However, the worst part is, as Marvel fans
know, that Hawkeye never even appeared in

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.300
Avengers: Infinity War!

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:46.790
So, instead of appearing in two of the biggest
films of 2018, Renner played tag with Jon

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:50.460
Hamm, Ed Helms, Hannibal Buress, and Jake
Johnson instead.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:54.950
Avengers 4 did shoot back-to-back with Infinity
War though, so hopefully he does have a decent

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:56.330
part in that movie.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:06:00.400
And, assuming Renner does want to return,
I think we'll see Brandt back in Mission:

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.780
Impossible 7.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.229
Speaking of which, although I feel Fallout
positioned CIA boss Erica Sloan as the likely

00:06:06.229 --> 00:06:10.830
successor to Hunley, Brandt did work closely
with Hunley in Rogue Nation, so it's possible

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.590
he could take over as the IMF's new Secretary.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:17.270
Though that probably wouldn't be the most
interesting role for his character and might

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:20.759
be disappointing to fans who'd like to see
him return to active duty.

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:25.020
A funny trailer moment was cut from the scene
when Benji, Ethan and Luther were waiting

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.800
to make the plutonium trade.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:27.849
"This is a bad idea."

00:06:27.849 --> 00:06:29.870
"Is it ever a good one?"

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:34.130
So, do you wish any of these deleted scenes
had been in the film?

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:38.250
And where does Fallout rank on your list of
favourite Mission: Impossible movies?

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.289
Let me know in the comments below.

